<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deskaheh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Levi General</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deskaheh, born Levi General, was a Cayuga chief and speaker of the traditional council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-nations-of-the-grand-river">Six Nations of the Grand River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. In 1923 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> on a Haudenosaunee passport to ask the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> to recognise the Six Nations as a sovereign people. The appeal failed, and in 1924 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> replaced the traditional council with an elected band council. He died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> State in 1925.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deskaheh.jpg" alt="Deskaheh" width="279" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Graphic (an illustrated weekly newspaper) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deskaheh_photo.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Levi General was born on 15 March 1873 and raised as a traditional Cayuga, taking an active part in longhouse ceremonies. Besides Cayuga, his first language, he spoke the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iroquoian-languages">Iroquoian languages</a>. He worked as a lumberjack in the Allegheny Mountains of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> until an accident sent him home, and he then farmed near Ohsweken on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-nations-of-the-grand-river">Six Nations Reserve</a>, where he married and had four daughters. In 1917 he became a hereditary Royaner of the Cayuga with the title Deskaheh, meaning more than eleven.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In August 1921 Deskaheh travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> with the attorney George P. Decker, the Six Nations' counsel, on a passport issued by the Confederacy, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> would have refused him leave to travel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>, then undersecretary for the colonies, said their petition should go back to the Canadian government. In 1922 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>' foreign minister, H. A. van Karnebeck, forwarded the petition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> built Royal Canadian Mounted Police barracks on Six Nations land and searched homes in 1923, Deskaheh sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> on 14 July 1923. He stayed eighteen months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, lecturing in Geneva, Bern, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, Lucerne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a> and Zurich on the obligations of the Two Row Wampum Treaty, and won support from Ireland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. The historian Laurence Hauptman judged that the lectures did not change British or Canadian positions. In October 1924 the RCMP dissolved the traditional government, taking documents and wampums, and Deskaheh left Geneva on 3 January 1925 without ever addressing the League.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last months in Rochester he gave his best-known speech on 10 March 1925 over a local radio station, saying of the policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> called it Indian Advancement and Washington Assimilation, while its victims called it tyranny. He died of pneumonia on 27 June 1925 at the home of Chief Clinton Rickard on the Tuscarora Reservation, his medicine man having been refused entry at the border. Two thousand mourners followed his casket on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-nations-of-the-grand-river">Six Nations Reserve</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deskaheh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1924 Canada imposed an elected band council at Six Nations, after the Cayuga chief <b>Deskaheh</b> had sought recognition from the League of Nations, but the traditional chiefs continue to meet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The Haudenosaunee government has issued its own passports since 1923, when it gave one to the Cayuga statesman <b>Deskaheh</b> to travel to the League of Nations.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-nations-of-the-grand-river">Six Nations of the Grand River 40</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.05,-80.12&amp;year=1906" aria-label="Six Nations of the Grand River 40 on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2616 635 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Deskaheh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3066 905h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3066 905h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.05,-80.12&amp;year=1906">What was here in 1906? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
